## Git Workflow

{{- if .is_work }}

**Branch naming:** All branches must follow Shortcut ticket naming conventions (e.g., `feature/sc-65682/improve-synthetic-data-script`). When creating branches, use the Shortcut-generated branch name.

**Commit messages:** Reference the Shortcut ticket ID in commits where relevant.

{{- else }}

**Branch naming:** Feature branches use informal descriptive names (e.g., `add-physics-system`, `fix-login-bug`). No ticket system - tasks tracked in TODO.md per project.

{{- end }}

**Commits:** NEVER commit without pausing for review, even if "accept edits" is enabled:

1. Show what was changed
2. Wait for explicit approval
3. Only then commit

**Exception:** If I explicitly say "commit this" or similar, proceed directly.

**Using the Worktree Manager**

worktree-manager.sh <issue#>-slug          # e.g. 5-fix-gdunit4-compile-errors
worktree-manager.sh --claude <issue#>-slug # same, then launches Claude Code in it (backgrounded)

- Run from any existing worktree directory (it locates the repo itself).
- Branch name convention varies by environment and possibly by project. At work, use --sc for shortcut integration, at home (e.g. Kardashev) look for instructions like [[kardashev-issue-branch-convention]]
- Auto-creates the branch from main, places the worktree as a sibling dir at /Users/evan/Documents/Development/Kardashev/<issue#>-slug, and symlinks any untracked .claude/ config and .env* files from the main worktree.
